atements are true?\n\nI. - ${\\sum }_{n=8}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is divergent\nII. - $12\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is divergent\nIII. - ${\\sum }_{n=8}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}+12\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is divergent" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the properties of convergent series:\n\n- If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }K\\cdot {a}_{n}=K\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is also convergent.\n\n- If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent and ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is convergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is convergent.\n\n- If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=k}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is also convergent.\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false. Removing a finite number of terms from a convergent series gives a convergent series.\n\n- Statement II is false. Any constant multiple of a convergent series gives a convergent series.\n\n- Statement III is false. The sum of two convergent series is convergent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of those listed.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258477 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d138d2c-3295-5eae-990d-42711f5792f1" :knowledge-point/title "Analyzing Statements Involving Sums of Convergent Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90b6a9cd-a484-529a-8eae-d44c92b7b4c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258479} {:answer/id #uuid "655f0d00-2dac-5a33-bc59-177ca995b0a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258480} {:answer/id #uuid "7666f9cc-0edd-5246-9564-e920e7845834" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258481} {:answer/id #uuid "dea017a7-0a8a-5608-9813-d66b7640c96d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258482} {:answer/id #uuid "7ad48b5d-91d0-5691-b2c6-c66e8b6cf618" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258483} {:answer/id #uuid "81bedfb0-99b6-5821-9374-1cda5e2e1598" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258484} {:answer/id #uuid "44f5addc-8dff-537c-abb2-d9cbb1c80671" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258485} {:answer/id #uuid "47cf5ddd-aac8-54a6-9cf6-8ff9e25b3283" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258486}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90b6a9cd-a484-529a-8eae-d44c92b7b4c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258479} :answer-field/id #uuid "8be80c42-2a5c-53e9-a30e-081f4ccc8721" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258478}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "294c68f1-5d11-5151-93f2-31f75aac6241" :question/math-academy-id "q-157096" :question/problem "Given that ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent and ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is convergent, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is convergent\nII. - $-9\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent\nIII. - ${\\sum }_{n=15}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is convergent" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the properties of convergent series:\n\n- If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }K\\cdot {a}_{n}=K\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is also convergent.\n\n- If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent and ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is convergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is convergent.\n\n- If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=k}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is also convergent.\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. The sum of two convergent series is convergent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Any constant multiple of a convergent series gives a convergent series.\n\n- Statement III is true. Removing a finite number of terms from a convergent series gives a convergent series.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258487 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d138d2c-3295-5eae-990d-42711f5792f1" :knowledge-point/title "Analyzing Statements Involving Sums of Convergent Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e030cded-26f1-5e3b-9d46-4d301b12cffb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258489} {:answer/id #uuid "08133c87-65a8-5b63-8453-50e84304f73f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258490} {:answer/id #uuid "cdadc521-ad65-5b9d-8732-d61558d541aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258491} {:answer/id #uuid "53bf4a29-7ee0-57cf-98cc-37448d24a7d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258492} {:answer/id #uuid "fc8fcaf8-586a-5faf-bfcb-cfd46f3c322c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258493} {:answer/id #uuid "2e9d461c-c1c4-51d0-a190-93261b2f1a0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258494} {:answer/id #uuid "ed84ca34-b06b-5dab-9bff-913989c60b0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258495} {:answer/id #uuid "9c1fa0df-840a-5edd-95b9-8a1d0ed49477" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258496}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e030cded-26f1-5e3b-9d46-4d301b12cffb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258489} :answer-field/id #uuid "97c7de96-9ab6-5150-97bd-53fbe2330f76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258488}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4e9d21a6-895c-56ef-b36c-707b55ffc68e" :question/math-academy-id "q-157099" :question/problem "Given that ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent and ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is convergent, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. - $11\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent\nII. - ${\\sum }_{n=16}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is convergent\nIII. - $11\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=16}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is convergent" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the properties of convergent series:\n\n- If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }K\\cdot {a}_{n}=K\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is also convergent.\n\n- If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent and ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is convergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is convergent.\n\n- If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=k}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is also convergent.\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Any constant multiple of a convergent series gives a convergent series.\n\n- Statement II is true. Removing a finite number of terms from a convergent series gives a convergent series.\n\n- Statement III is true. The sum of two convergent series is convergent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258497 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b18b8b49-3de3-54ba-8b4d-ca3c8a50c2c6" :knowledge-point/title "Analyzing Statements Involving Sums of Convergent and Divergent Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22494098-8b65-5fa2-b2be-110fdcb3be8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258499} {:answer/id #uuid "121bb038-9e31-5a1d-b52a-6d4ad07f1776" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258500} {:answer/id #uuid "70607e51-2474-5ba3-a376-fa8ab6de1919" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258501} {:answer/id #uuid "967558d8-749b-573a-9452-5b2811814032" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258502} {:answer/id #uuid "3753ca99-00a1-54cc-aba0-1684376b462e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258503} {:answer/id #uuid "1af3e68b-7ab7-5b91-aa8f-bc70efb6cd63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258504} {:answer/id #uuid "ea2380d0-c509-58f8-a973-f7d08f0df798" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258505} {:answer/id #uuid "ab973a9d-f335-5266-ab60-179071c03b3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258506}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22494098-8b65-5fa2-b2be-110fdcb3be8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258499} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ad3a4e2-a944-5a9b-86c6-00785b7a28c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258498}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f21f9a92-e265-5a42-858b-c3523a61288f" :question/math-academy-id "q-157136" :question/problem "Given that ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent and ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is divergent, which of the following statements are true? Assume that ${b}_{n}$ is well-defined for $n⩾1.$\n\nI. - $12\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent\nII. - $12\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is divergent\nIII. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{50}{b}_{n}$ is convergent" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the properties of divergent series:\n\n- If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is divergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }K\\cdot {a}_{n}$ is also divergent for $K\\ne 0.$\n\n- If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent and ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is divergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is divergent.\n\n- If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is divergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=k}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is also divergent.\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Any non-zero multiple of a convergent series gives a convergent series.\n\n- Statement II is true. The sum of a convergent series and a divergent series is divergent.\n\n- Statement III is true. Note that ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{50}{b}_{n}$ is a **finite series.** Therefore, ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{50}{b}_{n}$ is convergent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258611 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2c88884a-128a-5d46-bfbe-3773139ee11c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Years to Yield a Particular Amount"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6cca637b-6e4b-547f-830e-e71740039cfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "94" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271190} {:answer/id #uuid "bfb31ffd-a54b-5e41-aba9-dbb00f4801b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271191} {:answer/id #uuid "bbbb3b1f-c21a-5ec9-bea5-781d7e6c84d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "92" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271192} {:answer/id #uuid "28a7d820-63d5-5775-854a-aec6901da09b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "91" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271193} {:answer/id #uuid "c73d2d5c-7413-502e-83b7-cad25f15c822" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "93" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271194}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bfb31ffd-a54b-5e41-aba9-dbb00f4801b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271191} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a92211c-4256-58ca-9094-844c52002966" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274516}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c960aee5-3c8c-5166-a2ed-4e95f0b23d9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-166947" :question/problem "If you deposit $$12,000$ into an account with a $8%$ annual interest rate, compounded quarterly, how many years will it take until there is at least $$18,000$ in the account?" :question/worked-solution "The compound interest equation states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & ={P}_{0}{(1+\\frac{r}{n})}^{nt}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe annual interest is compounded quarterly, and there are $4$ quarters in one year, so $n=4.$ We also have an initial amount of ${P}_{0}=12,000$ and annual rate of $r=0.08.$\n\nTo find the number of years it will take until $P\\ge 18,000,$ we substitute these values into the formula and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P={P}_{0}{(1+\\frac{r}{n})}^{nt} & \\ge 18,000 \\\\ 12,000{(1+\\frac{0.08}{4})}^{4t} & \\ge 18,000 \\\\ (1+0.02)^{4t} & \\ge \\frac{18,000}{12,000} \\\\ (1.02)^{4t} & \\ge 1.5 \\\\ log\\,({1.02}^{4t}) & \\ge log\\,(1.5) \\\\ 4tlog\\,(1.02) & \\ge log\\,(1.5) \\\\ 4t & \\ge \\frac{log\\,(1.5)}{log\\,(1.02)} \\\\ 4t & \\ge 20.475… \\\\ t & \\ge \\frac{20.475…}{4}\\approx 5.12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember, payments are compounded quarterly, so we must choose either $5$ or $5.25$ years.\n\n- After $5$ years, there is still less than $$18,000$ in the account, because $5<5.12.$\n\n- After $5.25$ years, there will be more than $$18,000$ in the account, since $5.25>5.12.$\n\nTherefore, it will take $5.25$ years ($5$ years and $1$ quarter) until there is at least $$18,000$ in the account."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258759 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "89f65f58-bd3d-5c3f-b9e0-462d74d75b59" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Second Order Derivatives"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05012ae5-4eac-55c3-a687-b0d45b6a334e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271305} {:answer/id #uuid "739edd2c-777b-5b52-9aca-b25e2cbb2adb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60x^{2} − 8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271306} {:answer/id #uuid "40393c00-f7a0-51b3-9f8b-3d97042616d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72x+5\\operatorname{sin}x-4\\operatorname{cos}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271307} {:answer/id #uuid "df813580-c588-5300-9fb0-9b23c3f9215b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271308} {:answer/id #uuid "4699197a-cd80-5473-9f14-3f7b2c41a71d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20x^{3} − 8x − 7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271309}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40393c00-f7a0-51b3-9f8b-3d97042616d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72x+5\\operatorname{sin}x-4\\operatorname{cos}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271307} :answer-field/id #uuid "15cb1294-ac28-5bee-ac1e-a505da857a1a" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274539}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e0bfad45-7f5d-5462-bf0e-7422f149d562" :question/math-academy-id "q-1852" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{''}(x)$ for $f(x)=12{x}^{3}-5\\operatorname{sin}x+4\\operatorname{cos}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Computing the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & ={(12{x}^{3}-5\\operatorname{sin}x+4\\operatorname{cos}x)}^{'} &  \\\\  & =3\\cdot 12{x}^{2}-5(\\operatorname{cos}x)+4(-\\operatorname{sin}x) \\\\  & =36{x}^{2}-5\\operatorname{cos}x-4\\operatorname{sin}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{''}(x) & ={(36{x}^{2}-5\\operatorname{cos}x-4\\operatorname{sin}x)}^{'} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 36x-5(-\\operatorname{sin}x)-4(\\operatorname{cos}x) \\\\  & =72x+5\\operatorname{sin}x-4\\operatorname{cos}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258765 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6afb4058-908a-5983-9c1e-78ee3fbadf44" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Second Order Derivatives Using Leibnitz Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f416742e-d9ae-5a34-a172-3e2d4e547af2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "84x^{5} + 100x^{3} − 24x^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271315} {:answer/id #uuid "689e90ff-8234-5340-9483-be2f34cca8c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}x+30x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271316} {:answer/id #uuid "94073794-0bff-5d54-ad37-cee6c75992a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60x^{5} + 100x^{3} + (24)/(x^{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271317} {:answer/id #uuid "9e79e1a8-5d61-5e37-affb-e06c8bd3368f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "84x^{5} + 60x^{3} + (24)/(x^{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271318} {:answer/id #uuid "acb5b71d-439e-5fa5-87fc-fb0bbaef562d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "84x^{5} + 100x^{3} − (24)/(x^{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271319}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "689e90ff-8234-5340-9483-be2f34cca8c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}x+30x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271316} :answer-field/id #uuid "63c6f811-3a58-56f3-a958-6cb65ab8511b" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274541}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a9bb1b98-cc66-5ed7-8fea-7a253386cbb5" :question/math-academy-id "q-1873" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ for $y=-3\\operatorname{sin}x+5{x}^{3}-19.$" :question/worked-solution "Computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-3\\operatorname{sin}x+5{x}^{3}-19) \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{cos}x+3\\cdot 5{x}^{2}-0 \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{cos}x+15{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-3\\operatorname{cos}x+15{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-3(-\\operatorname{sin}x)+2\\cdot 15x \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}x+30x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259074 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f42eb9ad-cd67-5669-a3d9-b464810319e5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Amount of Money After Compound Interest Is Applied"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77e45620-0f2d-5aca-8e64-e3ddbffbc376" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(5809)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271587} {:answer/id #uuid "c69bfbe7-9c00-50f7-9775-96e6cdbbeddb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(5809)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271588} {:answer/id #uuid "21f9cda5-1190-5a63-b255-89df7bf1c8f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(5809)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271589} {:answer/id #uuid "1818c462-df73-5bd2-9764-7776cdbf5791" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(5809)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271590} {:answer/id #uuid "96c2c182-9669-5b7d-9269-3103e67ea972" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5809" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271591}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96c2c182-9669-5b7d-9269-3103e67ea972" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5809" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271591} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c161fc8-d333-5c7b-a78e-dd5f9429d81f" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274595}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e2127f05-8274-5b94-ada9-a82d39e1fd3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-26115" :question/problem "Mike has deposited $$5,000$ into an account paying $1%$ annual interest, compounded monthly. How much money will be in his account after $15$ years? Round your answer to the nearest dollar." :question/worked-solution "The compound interest equation states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & ={P}_{0}{(1+\\frac{r}{n})}^{nt}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe annual interest is compounded monthly, and there are $12$ months in one year, so $n=12.$ We also have an initial amount of ${P}_{0}=5,000,$ an annual interest of $r=0.01,$ and a length of $t=15$ years. Substituting these values into the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & ={P}_{0}{(1+\\frac{r}{n})}^{nt} \\\\  & =5,000{(1+\\frac{0.01}{12})}^{12(15)} \\\\  & =5,000(1+0.000\\,833…)^{180} \\\\  & =5,000(1.000\\,833…)^{180} \\\\  & =5,000(1.161\\,76…) \\\\  & \\approx 5,809.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, after $15$ years, Mike will have $$5,809$ in the account."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259077 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f42eb9ad-cd67-5669-a3d9-b464810319e5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Amount of Money After Compound Interest Is Applied"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fa94708-c2d8-5b0f-88e9-eeb63cb3629c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(117327)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271592} {:answer/id #uuid "58ac9522-066d-5a09-9e3b-1234729fa58d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(117327)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271593} {:answer/id #uuid "10c1a3da-947b-5e9c-9125-92f039a72c24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(117327)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271594} {:answer/id #uuid "a3d9bb78-2e79-5a0d-9afb-bfe0e658a951" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(117327)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271595} {:answer/id #uuid "2f57c3eb-6d48-5a94-a57c-2610f0e5c23e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "117327" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271596}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f57c3eb-6d48-5a94-a57c-2610f0e5c23e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "117327" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271596} :answer-field/id #uuid "f34e2b9e-def1-5416-a5d0-8572a2284a43" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274596}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bbc7d88e-dd0c-51cd-a26d-e7a5e1d7b81a" :question/math-academy-id "q-26182" :question/problem "A principal of $$102,000$ is invested in the stock market and earns interest at a rate of $2%$ per year, compounded daily. To the nearest dollar, how much will the investment with worth after $7$ years (ignore the leap years)?" :question/worked-solution "The compound interest equation states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & ={P}_{0}{(1+\\frac{r}{n})}^{nt}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe annual interest is compounded daily, and there are $365$ days in one year, so $n=365.$ We also have an initial amount of ${P}_{0}=102,000,$ an annual interest of $r=0.02,$ and a length of $t=7$ years. Substituting these values into the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & ={P}_{0}{(1+\\frac{r}{n})}^{nt} \\\\  & =102,000{(1+\\frac{0.02}{365})}^{365(7)} \\\\  & =102,000(1+0.000\\,054…)^{2,555} \\\\  & =102,000(1.000\\,054…)^{2,555} \\\\  & =102,000(1.150…) \\\\  & \\approx 117,327.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, after $7$ years, the value will be  $$117,327.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259080 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e04791d-e117-5a87-8491-1b2b5797f13a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Initial Deposit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d0d8b5c-41f8-56ce-b64d-3c0c0dd79b8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(31323)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271597} {:answer/id #uuid "3d3b8b96-b2f1-5796-aa78-2a2bc6a68e87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(31323)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271598} {:answer/id #uuid "ff1699fe-c610-5623-9a3d-3f31319134c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "31323" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271599} {:answer/id #uuid "fa738589-d64e-53e5-ab48-ffe68362e6fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(31323)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271600} {:answer/id #uuid "31c31381-badb-592d-8b2e-0699b61e4905" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(31323)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271601}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff1699fe-c610-5623-9a3d-3f31319134c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "31323" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271599} :answer-field/id #uuid "be94e39e-ed5c-5c0f-8537-a5fec10673ae" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274597}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "070b70b8-07a9-553e-acd0-2c4fadb162ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-26198" :question/problem "A savings account offers a $3%$ annual interest rate compounded daily. If the account currently has a balance of $$37,500,$ how much was in the account $6$ years ago (ignore the leap years)? Round your answer to the nearest dollar." :question/worked-solution "The compound interest equation states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & ={P}_{0}{(1+\\frac{r}{n})}^{nt}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe annual interest is compounded daily, and there are $365$ days in one year, so $n=365.$ We also have an annual rate of $r=0.03,$ and a final amount of $P=37,500$ after $t=6$ years. To find the initial deposit, ${P}_{0},$ we substitute these values into the formula and solve for ${P}_{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & ={P}_{0}{(1+\\frac{r}{n})}^{nt} \\\\ 37,500 & ={P}_{0}{(1+\\frac{0.03}{365})}^{365(6)} \\\\ 37,500 & ={P}_{0}(1.000\\,082…)^{2,190} \\\\ 37,500 & ={P}_{0}(1.197…) \\\\ {P}_{0} & =\\frac{37,500}{1.197…} \\\\ {P}_{0} & \\approx 31,323\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $6$ years ago, the savings account had an initial value of $$31,323.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259275 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "83423c03-d314-5f78-9acf-58cbb6f7774b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Unit Vector Parallel to a Given Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09d5a123-4fb6-521a-bcac-bb274ffdc3d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259277}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09d5a123-4fb6-521a-bcac-bb274ffdc3d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259277} :answer-field/id #uuid "c38b5a73-a839-59b5-814e-abb5f625b64d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259276} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d9b0dcd3-9de6-5cc0-ba38-d69ec3de0ab2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{8}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259279}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9b0dcd3-9de6-5cc0-ba38-d69ec3de0ab2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{8}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259279} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6d5b760-60d5-5012-bfa5-8667189279b1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259278}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8bd03f4e-4cf4-5f9e-99a0-8445e7cf14c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-281598" :question/problem "Given that $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$, find a unit vector that is parallel to $v.$\n\n|  |  | $\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{8}}$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $u=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-2}} |  |" :question/worked-solution "Remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{|\\,v\\,|}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,v\\,| & =\\sqrt{{v}_{x}^{2}+{v}_{y}^{2}+{v}_{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+0+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $v$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{v}{|\\,v\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{8}}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{8}}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{8}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{8}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259280 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "83423c03-d314-5f78-9acf-58cbb6f7774b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Unit Vector Parallel to a Given Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd6a2755-76c0-560b-8aec-2a7c46d85ca9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{65}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259282}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd6a2755-76c0-560b-8aec-2a7c46d85ca9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{65}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259282} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e02b4ab-23c6-5efe-a930-f01f07fd748f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259281} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eada3912-a2e9-5c0b-b04e-ba218d303a43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{65}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259284}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eada3912-a2e9-5c0b-b04e-ba218d303a43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{65}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259284} :answer-field/id #uuid "95498013-eef1-5685-805c-80ad36dd854e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259283}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "74cf4bef-d896-5bbd-b1b1-0cf31a04a44f" :question/math-academy-id "q-281604" :question/problem "Given that $w=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]$, find a unit vector that is parallel to $w.$\n\n|  |  | $\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{65}}$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $u=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-2}} |  |" :question/worked-solution "Remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{w}{|\\,w\\,|}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,w\\,| & =\\sqrt{{w}_{x}^{2}+{w}_{y}^{2}+{w}_{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-5)^{2}+{6}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+25+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{65}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $w$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{w}{|\\,w\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{65}}w \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{65}}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{65}} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{65}} \\\\ \\frac{6}{\\sqrt{65}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259285 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1916efa2-6a98-59a5-858c-c4fe64d8e8bc" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Magnitude of a Vector Given the Coordinates of its Endpoints"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5225462e-ca00-5ed8-a0f7-7975997fdf05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{59}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259287}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5225462e-ca00-5ed8-a0f7-7975997fdf05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{59}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259287} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5a1c15f-128f-5942-80b0-932025fc61d5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259286}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1a3261c4-18b3-5911-b162-0718243641dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-281692" :question/problem "Let $R(0,6,-2)$ and $S(-5,1,1).$ Find $|\\overset{\\to }{SR}|.$\n\n$|\\overset{\\to }{SR}|=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OR} & =r=6j-2k, \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{OS} & =s=-5i+j+k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing $\\overset{\\to }{SR}$ as the difference of position vectors, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{SR} & =\\overset{\\to }{OR}-\\overset{\\to }{OS} \\\\  & =r-s \\\\  & =(6j-2k)-(-5i+j+k) \\\\  & =5i+5j-3k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\overset{\\to }{SR}| & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{5}^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25+25+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{59}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259298 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "322168bb-52e3-5352-a2e3-c5bdbf2bc13e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Higher Order Derivatives"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4e084f5-d0e1-54fd-a220-ae39eb2854eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6)/(x^{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271647} {:answer/id #uuid "a20eaec6-b443-514e-8963-f0c6b34e48fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− (6)/(x^{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271648} {:answer/id #uuid "a4c2a257-ec2d-5b72-b659-afcb2c7c64fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271649} {:answer/id #uuid "5bab00bc-339d-5dfd-b320-ed101697d736" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(18)/(x^{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271650} {:answer/id #uuid "268e66dd-cb2e-5336-9ab4-da4051aecf7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− (6)/(x^{4}) + 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271651}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4c2a257-ec2d-5b72-b659-afcb2c7c64fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271649} :answer-field/id #uuid "179b4d31-67af-5f10-8fd7-7f4c29cabffb" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274607}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f5dd3c3b-72ac-507f-a820-ce546a04182e" :question/math-academy-id "q-28551" :question/problem "Given $y=7{x}^{2}-1+\\frac{1}{2x}$, find $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite the function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(x) & =7{x}^{2}-1+\\frac{1}{2x} \\\\  & =7{x}^{2}-1+\\frac{{x}^{-1}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(7{x}^{2}-1+\\frac{{x}^{-1}}{2}) \\\\  & =7(2)x-0+\\frac{(-1){x}^{-2}}{2} \\\\  & =14x-\\frac{{x}^{-2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(14x-\\frac{{x}^{-2}}{2}) \\\\  & =14-\\frac{(-2){x}^{-3}}{2} \\\\  & =14+{x}^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, computing the third derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(14+{x}^{-3}) \\\\  & =0+(-3){x}^{-4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259400 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9102c1c-1c38-58dd-9932-b04bd24a6846" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a General Logarithm Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75455967-ab5b-5fa9-a900-8596d3bd9923" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{(6x-7)\\operatorname{ln}(5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259402}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75455967-ab5b-5fa9-a900-8596d3bd9923" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{(6x-7)\\operatorname{ln}(5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259402} :answer-field/id #uuid "79303664-97ab-5458-84fe-059bbf54a7cf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259401}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "63271a66-41d7-583a-8800-b8588316b76a" :question/math-academy-id "q-310148" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={log}_{5}(7-6x),$ find ${f}^{'}(x).$\n\n${f}^{'}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\n{f}^{'}(x)={log}_{5}(u),\\,u=7-6x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u\\operatorname{ln}(5)},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-6.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u\\operatorname{ln}(5)}\\cdot (-6) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(7-6x)\\operatorname{ln}(5)}\\cdot (-6) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{(6x-7)\\operatorname{ln}(5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259403 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "322168bb-52e3-5352-a2e3-c5bdbf2bc13e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Higher Order Derivatives"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f1fcdbc-a080-57f7-ba71-08ea4d2d53d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{{x}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259405}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f1fcdbc-a080-57f7-ba71-08ea4d2d53d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{{x}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259405} :answer-field/id #uuid "928663e7-ec6e-52d7-bce4-edd3e45dbad7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259404}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c6d68558-2aaa-5e27-875e-de85a290b654" :question/math-academy-id "q-310170" :question/problem "Given $g(x)=4{x}^{2}+6x-\\frac{2}{x},$ we have\n\n${g}^{‴}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite the function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & =4{x}^{2}+6x-\\frac{2}{x} \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}+6x-2{x}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{2}+6x-2{x}^{-1}) \\\\  & =8x+6+2{x}^{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{''}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8x+6+2{x}^{-2}) \\\\  & =8-4{x}^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, computing the third derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{‴}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8-4{x}^{-3}) \\\\  & =12{x}^{-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{{x}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259409 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b194bf2f-feaa-5656-a3ee-0ff466a39e77" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Product Containing a Trigonometric Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c857ebdc-bf0f-5abb-b2fe-658d35af257b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{3}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}(3)\\operatorname{sin}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259411}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c857ebdc-bf0f-5abb-b2fe-658d35af257b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{3}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}(3)\\operatorname{sin}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259411} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9a0a841-49fd-5c99-8a94-f01b93664851" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259410}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3d49772e-f3e5-5d1d-a237-9dc33783e3e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-310188" :question/problem "Differentiate ${3}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}(x).$\n\n$({3}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}(x))^{'}=$\n{{field-1}} $+\\,{3}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}(x)$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)={3}^{x}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{sin}(x).$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({3}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}(x))^{'} & ={u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x) \\\\  & =({3}^{x})^{'}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}(x)+{3}^{x}\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}(x))^{'} \\\\  & =({3}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}(3))(\\operatorname{sin}(x))+({3}^{x})(\\operatorname{cos}(x)) \\\\  & ={3}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}(3)\\operatorname{sin}(x)+{3}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259415 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee64135e-9b9f-5429-a4df-efa522042b83" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Product  at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8007a71-d8c2-5e56-a864-fe578745b1a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271739} {:answer/id #uuid "7820ba67-447a-58a5-bee2-2c2d4814cd79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271740} {:answer/id #uuid "c6424cf6-cbfd-5973-9b43-e944281d3dd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271741} {:answer/id #uuid "601574a5-1487-5150-a572-85b58e696fca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271742} {:answer/id #uuid "c1b31d66-25a2-5a64-91fe-e1665c4f461d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271743}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7820ba67-447a-58a5-bee2-2c2d4814cd79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271740} :answer-field/id #uuid "15a83a0f-bc4b-5aae-ac24-dfceb90f1b9c" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274627}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5c9eb90c-59cb-57bc-b62b-90c5bc3c0020" :question/math-academy-id "q-31226" :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{tan}x,$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\pi .$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=3{x}^{2}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{tan}x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6x,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={sec}^{2}x.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =(3{x}^{2})({sec}^{2}x)+(\\operatorname{tan}x)(6x) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}{sec}^{2}x+6x\\operatorname{tan}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\pi $ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{x=\\pi } & =3{x}^{2}{sec}^{2}x+6x\\operatorname{tan}x{|}_{x=\\pi } \\\\  & =3(\\pi )^{2}{sec}^{2}(\\pi )+6(\\pi )\\operatorname{tan}(\\pi ) \\\\  & =3{\\pi }^{2}\\cdot 1+6\\pi \\cdot 0 \\\\  & =3{\\pi }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $3{\\pi }^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259525 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b194bf2f-feaa-5656-a3ee-0ff466a39e77" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Product Containing a Trigonometric Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43184841-49d1-5070-bdf5-0a234bfec24b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{tan}(x)-2)\\operatorname{sin}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259527}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "43184841-49d1-5070-bdf5-0a234bfec24b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{tan}(x)-2)\\operatorname{sin}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259527} :answer-field/id #uuid "c39ff20e-51e9-56a7-837f-d05eb8d71985" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259526}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "878043a0-10cb-5659-bc30-ca3c5f00ae2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-319402" :question/problem "Differentiate $(2-\\operatorname{tan}(x))\\operatorname{cos}(x)$\n\n$((2-\\operatorname{tan}(x))\\operatorname{cos}(x))^{'}=-{sec}^{2}(x)\\operatorname{cos}(x)\\,+$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)=2-\\operatorname{tan}(x)$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{cos}(x).$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}((2-\\operatorname{tan}(x))\\operatorname{cos}(x))^{'} & ={u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x) \\\\  & =(2-\\operatorname{tan}(x))^{'}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}(x)+(2-\\operatorname{tan}(x))\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}(x))^{'} \\\\  & =(-{sec}^{2}(x))(\\operatorname{cos}(x))+(2-\\operatorname{tan}(x))(-\\operatorname{sin}(x)) \\\\  & =-{sec}^{2}(x)\\operatorname{cos}(x)+(\\operatorname{tan}(x)-2)\\operatorname{sin}(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259528 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f15a60dd-f6a7-577d-bdbc-148032de94c8" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Product Containing an Exponential or Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "352d9a06-5c86-5ad5-a31c-625b0ff74a53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{5}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}(5)\\operatorname{sin}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259530}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "352d9a06-5c86-5ad5-a31c-625b0ff74a53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{5}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}(5)\\operatorname{sin}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259530} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9a3a4c7-b18f-5795-8484-c3728f71e3f5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259529}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "458f8052-b021-5a86-a091-16ca938dd05f" :question/math-academy-id "q-319421" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={5}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}(x),$ find ${f}^{'}(x).$\n\n${f}^{'}(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $+\\,{5}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}(x)$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x)={5}^{x}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{sin}(x).$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & ={({5}^{x})}^{'}\\operatorname{sin}(x)+{5}^{x}{(\\operatorname{sin}(x))}^{'} \\\\  & =({5}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}(5))\\operatorname{sin}(x)+{5}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}(x)) \\\\  & ={5}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}(5)\\operatorname{sin}(x)+{5}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259543 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee64135e-9b9f-5429-a4df-efa522042b83" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Product  at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48cf976f-c492-5116-a478-b5c3d499c7bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259545}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48cf976f-c492-5116-a478-b5c3d499c7bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259545} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c4079ac-5969-5352-90bf-234b527c5450" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259544}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dd0e54ab-23dd-5fbf-99e4-afe94b73a3d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-319825" :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=(x-1)\\operatorname{ln}(2x+1),$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=0$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=x-1$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{ln}(2x+1).$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{2x+1}.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =(x-1)\\cdot \\frac{2}{2x+1}+\\operatorname{ln}(2x+1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x-1)}{2x+1}+\\operatorname{ln}(2x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=0$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=0} & =\\frac{2(0-1)}{2(0)+1}+\\operatorname{ln}(2(0)+1) \\\\  & =(-2)+\\operatorname{ln}(1) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259546 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f15a60dd-f6a7-577d-bdbc-148032de94c8" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Product Containing an Exponential or Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c5ff6e9-0b3c-5118-9120-380862efdc1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259548}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c5ff6e9-0b3c-5118-9120-380862efdc1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259548} :answer-field/id #uuid "7dc5092f-6845-56f1-ac03-ff53f1048789" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259547}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1572c639-23d1-5637-b129-4ba470408d68" :question/math-academy-id "q-319909" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={x}^{2}{e}^{2x},$ find ${f}^{'}(x).$\n\n${f}^{'}(x)=2x{e}^{2x}\\,+$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x)={x}^{2}$ and $v(x)={e}^{2x}.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =({x}^{2})^{'}{e}^{2x}+{x}^{2}({e}^{2x})^{'} \\\\  & =(2x)({e}^{2x})+({x}^{2})(2{e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =2x{e}^{2x}+2{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259858 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a71301e9-bc7f-53b9-900e-c3bd37f96b03" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating the Product of a Constant and the Natural Logarithm Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99a9c5a7-38b1-50a3-99d9-a4c102af76d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259860}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99a9c5a7-38b1-50a3-99d9-a4c102af76d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259860} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a308953-a288-52bd-a0ef-9deb75f23b4f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259859}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "21582e9d-02bc-5764-84aa-8f2d1392331f" :question/math-academy-id "q-339941" :question/problem "Given that $y=-11\\operatorname{ln}x$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The derivative of the natural logarithm is the reciprocal function:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}x)=\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nWe apply our formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-11\\operatorname{ln}x) \\\\  & =-11\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}x) \\\\  & =-11\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{11}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259866 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "83ce3c28-857b-5538-b362-cf52526e3688" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Sum Containing a Natural Logarithm Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dbff73c0-4107-5d2d-80ee-2e309969accb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{x}+\\pi {x}^{\\pi -1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259868}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dbff73c0-4107-5d2d-80ee-2e309969accb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{x}+\\pi {x}^{\\pi -1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259868} :answer-field/id #uuid "34024961-12fc-58f9-a76e-e8aa412781dd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259867}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "56a1c713-cc3d-50c8-8cb6-4fdd7363b496" :question/math-academy-id "q-339973" :question/problem "Given that $y=2\\operatorname{ln}x+{x}^{\\pi }+1$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\operatorname{ln}x+{x}^{\\pi }+1) \\\\  & =2(\\frac{1}{x})+\\pi {x}^{\\pi -1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x}+\\pi {x}^{\\pi -1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259869 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5856f3cc-5423-5a97-b2fd-7f44e0797ec7" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Logarithmic Function With a General Base"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b574247-6baf-54d0-8f4a-cab77cc1c36c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8-\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{ln}3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259871}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b574247-6baf-54d0-8f4a-cab77cc1c36c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8-\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{ln}3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259871} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee0c8a1e-b0e6-5071-94f7-653e925f1fa1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259870}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8da817ac-8d36-5248-8a56-e3e3305933b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-339989" :question/problem "Given the function $y=4{x}^{2}-6{log}_{3}x,$ find the instantaneous rate of change at $x=1.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change at $x=1$ is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To differentiate a logarithm with base $a,$ we use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({log}_{a}x)=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}a}.\n$$\n\nTo find the instantaneous rate of change at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nTaking the derivative we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{2}-6{log}_{3}x) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2x-6\\cdot \\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}3} \\\\  & =8x-\\frac{6}{x\\operatorname{ln}3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change at $x=1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(8x-\\frac{6}{x\\operatorname{ln}3})|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =8(1)-\\frac{6}{1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}3} \\\\  & =8-\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{ln}3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259872 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5856f3cc-5423-5a97-b2fd-7f44e0797ec7" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Logarithmic Function With a General Base"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ab0884e-e31a-5ed4-a2b3-d2fad90cbba3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}(7)}-\\frac{3}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259874}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ab0884e-e31a-5ed4-a2b3-d2fad90cbba3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}(7)}-\\frac{3}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259874} :answer-field/id #uuid "49b73348-7610-5233-9840-8f3bef55321f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259873}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1b0635f8-e7ae-5a1b-959e-70b659ecfb4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-339997" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={log}_{7}x-3\\operatorname{ln}x$, find ${f}^{'}(x).$\n\n${f}^{'}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To differentiate a logarithm with base $a,$ we use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({log}_{a}x)=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}a}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({log}_{7}x-3\\operatorname{ln}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({log}_{7}x)-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}(7)}-3(\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}(7)}-\\frac{3}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259875 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cef7fc21-23f4-5d6a-a208-9963ef23c784" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Slope of the Tangent Line of a Logarithmic Function at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bdd5f721-f706-5012-82b2-dd84492aa8b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259877}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bdd5f721-f706-5012-82b2-dd84492aa8b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259877} :answer-field/id #uuid "9364ab94-e028-59cc-bb87-1b9ee5e2b212" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259876}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "84b41208-fbbb-5c93-b4f6-118635b12b3e" :question/math-academy-id "q-340014" :question/problem "Given the function $y=5{x}^{2}+6\\operatorname{ln}x,$ the slope of the tangent line at $x=3$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the slope of the tangent at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nTaking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{x}^{2}+6\\operatorname{ln}x) \\\\  & =5\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+6\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}x) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 2x+6\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =10x+\\frac{6}{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=3$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=3} & ={(10x+\\frac{6}{x})|}_{x=3} \\\\  & =10\\cdot 3+\\frac{6}{3} \\\\  & =32\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259878 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cef7fc21-23f4-5d6a-a208-9963ef23c784" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Slope of the Tangent Line of a Logarithmic Function at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7efa9c47-eb42-5217-9b01-9324dffcf05f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}-18\\operatorname{ln}3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259880}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7efa9c47-eb42-5217-9b01-9324dffcf05f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}-18\\operatorname{ln}3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259880} :answer-field/id #uuid "93c1c169-e1a5-562c-9421-a536a3e6f150" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259879}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c733d8c4-483f-51cd-af63-0208432f9f16" :question/math-academy-id "q-340022" :question/problem "Given the function $y=5\\operatorname{ln}x-2\\cdot {3}^{x},$ the slope of the tangent line at $x=2$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the slope of the tangent at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nTaking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5\\operatorname{ln}x-2\\cdot {3}^{x}) \\\\  & =5\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}x)-2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({3}^{x}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}-2\\cdot {3}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}3 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{x}-2\\operatorname{ln}3\\cdot {3}^{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=2$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=2} & ={(\\frac{5}{x}-2\\operatorname{ln}3\\cdot {3}^{x})|}_{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}3\\cdot {3}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}-18\\operatorname{ln}3\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259884 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cef7fc21-23f4-5d6a-a208-9963ef23c784" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Slope of the Tangent Line of a Logarithmic Function at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ecadcc24-e607-5843-a6b4-9068f95f75d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{e}^{2}+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259886}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ecadcc24-e607-5843-a6b4-9068f95f75d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{e}^{2}+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259886} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6bbf671-b44c-59c0-9d96-625daffa19cb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259885}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2238b6dc-d73b-58d3-b7fe-799bc2a1f221" :question/math-academy-id "q-340109" :question/problem "Given the function $y=-3{e}^{x}+2{log}_{4}x,$ the slope of the tangent line at $x=2$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the slope of the tangent at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nTaking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-3{e}^{x}+2{log}_{4}x) \\\\  & =-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({log}_{4}x) \\\\  & =-3{e}^{x}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}4} \\\\  & =-3{e}^{x}+\\frac{2}{x\\operatorname{ln}4}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=2$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=2} & ={(-3{e}^{x}+\\frac{2}{x\\operatorname{ln}4})|}_{x=2} \\\\  & =-3{e}^{2}+\\frac{2}{2\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}4} \\\\  & =-3{e}^{2}+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}4}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259887 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc978820-517c-5885-b31b-ab6f9650d57a" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Quotient of Two Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e4931bc9-bc91-5131-a954-bdb1771462eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259889}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4931bc9-bc91-5131-a954-bdb1771462eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259889} :answer-field/id #uuid "263f54f4-c409-5d72-ae68-c6f60c986993" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259888}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "94125d6a-8fad-58e8-9ce8-c6017b8b43a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-340290" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}$, find ${f}^{'}(x).$\n\n${f}^{'}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The numerator and denominator are $u(x)=2$ and $v(x)={x}^{2},$ respectively. Their derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{'}(x) & =0, \\\\ {v}^{'}(x) & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0\\cdot {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2x}{{({x}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4x}{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259893 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c547746-49c8-5a4a-85fe-c6b75493a354" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Quotient Containing Other Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b4bc067-52c1-585d-8b3e-e01f0fb731fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{{e}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259895}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b4bc067-52c1-585d-8b3e-e01f0fb731fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{{e}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259895} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5516765-62a1-566d-a1a7-d1e93e58037d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259894}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "876d6c3c-d8bf-5b17-84ad-fecbaeadd41d" :question/math-academy-id "q-340318" :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=\\frac{x}{{e}^{3x}},$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=1.$\n\nThe slope at $x=1$ is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "In this case, the expression for $y$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)=x$ and $v(x)={e}^{3x}.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{(x)}^{'}\\cdot {e}^{3x}-x\\cdot {({e}^{3x})}^{'}}{{({e}^{3x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot {e}^{3x}-x\\cdot 3{e}^{3x}}{{({e}^{3x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3x}(1-3x)}{{({e}^{3x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-3x}{{e}^{3x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=1$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}\\,(1) & =\\frac{1-3(1)}{{e}^{3(1)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{{e}^{3}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{e}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line at $x=1$ is $-\\frac{2}{{e}^{3}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259896 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af269509-92c0-5a82-b828-e8626f8e8eda" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Quotient Using Leibniz Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43f21534-dc51-584d-bce2-28232b621df8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{sec}^{2}x-2\\operatorname{tan}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259898}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "43f21534-dc51-584d-bce2-28232b621df8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{sec}^{2}x-2\\operatorname{tan}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259898} :answer-field/id #uuid "acd4aa7d-7e27-5239-b341-720c28271e92" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259897}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b4792b81-53a1-5620-92e3-e7ad1403e4ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-340336" :question/problem "Given that $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}x}{{x}^{2}}$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\cdot ($\n{{field-1}}\n$).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=\\operatorname{tan}x$ and $v={x}^{2}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & ={sec}^{2}x\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}-u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}}{{v}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}\\cdot {sec}^{2}x-\\operatorname{tan}x\\cdot 2x}{{({x}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x(x{sec}^{2}x-2\\operatorname{tan}x)}{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\cdot (\\,x{sec}^{2}x-2\\operatorname{tan}x\\,).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259899 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af269509-92c0-5a82-b828-e8626f8e8eda" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Quotient Using Leibniz Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a0b4d1e-797e-5ae7-9daa-2d05442e5018" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x{e}^{x}}{(x+1)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259901}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a0b4d1e-797e-5ae7-9daa-2d05442e5018" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x{e}^{x}}{(x+1)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259901} :answer-field/id #uuid "efa6c8ef-b590-53a3-958a-ec1a9341ec79" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259900}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5ffdc549-0c35-5d99-abd9-dd981b4eb66f" :question/math-academy-id "q-340340" :question/problem "Given that $y=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x+1},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $u={e}^{x}$ and $v=x+1.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{x}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}-u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}}{{v}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)\\cdot {e}^{x}-{e}^{x}\\cdot 1}{(x+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}(x+1-1)}{(x+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x{e}^{x}}{(x+1)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259921 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4ac7f7e-1e07-57d7-a82a-d6545588c2de" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Natural Logarithm Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30fe750a-8a77-5618-a1c1-805db7e6d8ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{30{x}^{5}}{3-5{x}^{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259923}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30fe750a-8a77-5618-a1c1-805db7e6d8ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{30{x}^{5}}{3-5{x}^{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259923} :answer-field/id #uuid "6785800d-0d72-5219-a1cd-161bd26a5334" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259922}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3893bba9-7764-50a8-af7d-ead40b8716aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-341676" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}(3-5{x}^{6})$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=\\operatorname{ln}u,\\,u=3-5{x}^{6}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-30{x}^{5}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot (-30{x}^{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3-5{x}^{6}}\\cdot (-30{x}^{5}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{30{x}^{5}}{3-5{x}^{6}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259927 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9102c1c-1c38-58dd-9932-b04bd24a6846" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a General Logarithm Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62d9a6df-a59b-5467-8482-d97d363a14c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}(10)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259929}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62d9a6df-a59b-5467-8482-d97d363a14c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}(10)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259929} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e6b75f3-2dff-5d03-a672-54edd32423c2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259928}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "40b99611-d9a2-5783-9644-833a14c4a6de" :question/math-academy-id "q-341688" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\operatorname{log}(8x),$ find ${f}^{'}(x).$\n\n${f}^{'}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\n{f}^{'}(x)=\\operatorname{log}(u),\\,u=8x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u\\operatorname{ln}(10)},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=8.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u\\operatorname{ln}(10)}\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{8x\\operatorname{ln}(10)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{8}}{\\cancel{8}x\\operatorname{ln}(10)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}(10)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259995 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6afb4058-908a-5983-9c1e-78ee3fbadf44" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Second Order Derivatives Using Leibnitz Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fddbd78d-618d-59ac-a246-c3c03921c672" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14{x}^{6}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259997}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fddbd78d-618d-59ac-a246-c3c03921c672" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14{x}^{6}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259997} :answer-field/id #uuid "0369b864-2326-59d6-ba80-f0acde852e7e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259996}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0d1b276d-d31e-5087-ae03-2c3bbfd6a8d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-342868" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ for $y=\\frac{{x}^{8}}{4}+x-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}}.$\n\n$\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{8}}{4}+x-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{8}}{4}+x-4{x}^{-1/2}) \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{7}}{4}+1-(-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot 4{x}^{-3/2} \\\\  & =2{x}^{7}+1+2{x}^{-3/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{7}+1+2{x}^{-3/2}) \\\\  & =7\\cdot 2{x}^{6}+0+(-\\frac{3}{2})\\cdot 2{x}^{-5/2} \\\\  & =14{x}^{6}-3{x}^{-5/2} \\\\  & =14{x}^{6}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260098 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cfe5a9ca-d045-53ae-bc69-fff9dfbdc439" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a General Partial Sum of an Infinite Arithmetic Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2831a8e-cd46-5bcf-9e6e-8250017aa423" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k(2k+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260100}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2831a8e-cd46-5bcf-9e6e-8250017aa423" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k(2k+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260100} :answer-field/id #uuid "74bd2c09-e5e2-5529-a707-2f3c847d99ce" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260099}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "386438bb-70e7-5f64-bd73-4f9e34789bb2" :question/math-academy-id "q-349081" :question/problem "Find the $k$th partial sum ${s}_{k}$ of the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(4n-1).$ Express the answer in terms of the parameter $k.$\n\n${s}_{k}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{n}=4n-1.$ If we compute the first few terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =3 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =7 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =11,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we see that this is an arithmetic sequence with common difference $d=4$ and ${a}_{1}=3.$\n\nWe can compute the $k$th partial sum of an arithmetic series using the formula\n\n$$\n{s}_{k}=\\frac{k}{2}({a}_{1}+{a}_{k}).\n$$\n\nSince ${a}_{k}=4k-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{k} & =\\frac{k}{2}(3+(4k-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{2}(4k+2) \\\\  & =k(2k+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260107 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4dff2ea-a90b-5618-82fc-647a40be0c46" :knowledge-point/title "Convergence Parameters for p-Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e15372f-2cd6-5b4e-bd26-c10465a76a60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260109}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e15372f-2cd6-5b4e-bd26-c10465a76a60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260109} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6b64096-01fd-5615-9728-858bd0f7a199" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260108} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e487520-ea7e-523f-8ec4-434ba60d4177" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260111}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e487520-ea7e-523f-8ec4-434ba60d4177" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260111} :answer-field/id #uuid "abb9082c-0c1e-56c4-b330-c485b0848485" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260110}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1af68890-6058-5926-ba93-36a83ef50f1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-349269" :question/problem "For which values of $q$ does the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{-7-4q}}$ converge?\n\n$q$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "A $p$-series is any series that takes the form\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{p}},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. The $p$-series test states that\n\n- if $p>1,$ then the series converges, while\n\n- if $p\\le 1,$ then the series diverges.\n\nComparing our series to the $p$-series, we have\n\n$$\np=-7-4q.\n$$\n\nAccording to the $p$-series test, the given series converges if\n\n$$\n-7-4q>1\\,⟹\\,q<-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent for $q<-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260511 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5468f23-98bd-52d9-ba21-85e5eea058a9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Domain and Range of a Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc795347-216b-5e3d-9f38-2520ca1c5ee4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y < 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272271} {:answer/id #uuid "2000c09e-e222-5bfb-9188-3da4ee1b1e97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y < 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272272} {:answer/id #uuid "753d1f0f-5512-54d0-be90-1c6af9ff9f3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272273} {:answer/id #uuid "003aadad-1c0f-5087-81a7-3e70f2f7364a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y > 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272274} {:answer/id #uuid "ba06da71-23c1-5a35-9d52-7797e58b909f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272275}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "753d1f0f-5512-54d0-be90-1c6af9ff9f3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272273} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb75de5f-788b-5152-bb1a-a95349cc0025" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274734}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1a14db00-97f9-5ed3-bfa8-49652c2e587c" :question/math-academy-id "q-47777" :question/problem "What's the domain of $y={log}_{3}x?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y={log}_{3}x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/c1b7274ff6a0887efcaa27bc44db67934d425c0ca83a19fc761a2cccc72436f6.png)\n\nThe domain of $y={log}_{3}x$ is $x\\in (0,\\infty )$ and the range is $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x>0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260514 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5468f23-98bd-52d9-ba21-85e5eea058a9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Domain and Range of a Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c64377b-74c7-5cb8-b25f-a32a4ee66788" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[0,\\infty)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272276} {:answer/id #uuid "a041d7c5-bebd-5ece-97da-20609af943e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-\\infty,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272277} {:answer/id #uuid "3dbf3c7a-b031-5b25-9868-6c9237978962" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All real numbers" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272278} {:answer/id #uuid "f0bbd3cd-8978-5b39-b5e8-f0e730cb71e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,\\infty)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272279}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3dbf3c7a-b031-5b25-9868-6c9237978962" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All real numbers" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272278} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b559394-069f-5659-8b7e-521e96a7cd9a" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274735}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f7b6a962-a925-5eb4-8eb3-c093275b7ef1" :question/math-academy-id "q-47778" :question/problem "What's the range of $f(x)={log}_{9}x?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y={log}_{9}x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/40664f83ba162a93ed06970798d37798db90b85c21d44a9ed20f338df4e1b4e1.png)\n\nThe domain of $y={log}_{9}x$ is $x\\in (0,\\infty )$ and the range is $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All real numbers.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260637 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b18b8b49-3de3-54ba-8b4d-ca3c8a50c2c6" :knowledge-point/title "Analyzing Statements Involving Sums of Convergent and Divergent Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb78b81a-08e0-5883-b8c1-d2b3762bd274" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260639} {:answer/id #uuid "c88b193c-1e36-53f0-bba8-c3b41432dc35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260640} {:answer/id #uuid "2950368e-f30f-54a6-9cbc-e39d2b227801" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260641} {:answer/id #uuid "9764d03e-d2a5-51c2-b135-b591e8355b42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260642} {:answer/id #uuid "ddf52af7-4622-5274-b8d9-df77922937e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260643} {:answer/id #uuid "4be3504a-0f66-5048-9275-871a324af799" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260644} {:answer/id #uuid "5c5027dd-4dbf-56e5-b293-30e24a303a20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260645} {:answer/id #uuid "13f34607-fa97-57d2-b597-fa8c4bd337b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260646}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9764d03e-d2a5-51c2-b135-b591e8355b42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260642} :answer-field/id #uuid "c39958ab-9a75-5986-89db-259aee63a2ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260638}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9f068f64-42c9-5eab-a9dd-69dbbca65fcb" :question/math-academy-id "q-49836" :question/problem "Given that ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is divergent and ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is convergent, which of the following statements are true? Assume that ${a}_{n}$ is well-defined for $n⩾1.$\n\nI. - $\\frac{1}{10\\,000}\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is divergent\nII. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{8}{a}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is divergent\nIII. - ${\\sum }_{n=10}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the properties of divergent series:\n\n- If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is divergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }K\\cdot {a}_{n}$ is also divergent for $K\\ne 0.$\n\n- If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent and ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is divergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is divergent.\n\n- If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is divergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=k}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is also divergent.\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Any non-zero multiple of a divergent series gives a divergent series.\n\n- Statement II is false. Note that ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{8}{a}_{n}$ is a **finite series.** Therefore, ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{8}{a}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is convergent.\n\n- Statement III is false. Removing a finite number of terms from a divergent series gives a divergent series.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260669 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6051f35-c390-57ba-8448-40f53f11c1c1" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Which Series Converge From a List"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e82e0a94-0ef1-5341-a309-036af807e925" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260671} {:answer/id #uuid "d17dd7b5-7079-5e55-a247-27b2c0ea73e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260672} {:answer/id #uuid "ef7b6a8d-bcfe-50b3-a209-f5009fafc7bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260673} {:answer/id #uuid "b0008a49-cef9-5b3e-8b9e-c5f691191a5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260674} {:answer/id #uuid "0d405ad9-1854-5b2f-ba5e-5a63f59fac5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260675} {:answer/id #uuid "233a6e40-88e2-5eb9-86a7-69a846a8e298" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260676} {:answer/id #uuid "66bff3b0-0197-5440-8606-f7cf19285068" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260677} {:answer/id #uuid "4a02105c-bb09-5f55-963a-149b9d550997" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260678}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0008a49-cef9-5b3e-8b9e-c5f691191a5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260674} :answer-field/id #uuid "f719330c-8cad-5dd6-8388-518d937a28d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260670}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a897110c-16a9-5e34-8f8f-61673b309ea9" :question/math-academy-id "q-50629" :question/problem "Which of the following series are convergent?\n\nI. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n\\sqrt{{n}^{3}}}$\nII. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{\\sqrt[9]{{n}^{2}}}$\nIII. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{{n}^{3}}}{2{n}^{2}}$" :question/worked-solution "We analyze each series in turn.\n\n- First, we look at ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n\\sqrt{{n}^{3}}}.$ Rewriting this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n\\sqrt{{n}^{3}}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n\\cdot {n}^{3/2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{5/2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{5}{2}.$ Since $p>1,$ the series converges by the $p$-series test.\n\n- For the second series, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{\\sqrt[9]{{n}^{2}}}=8\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2/9}},\n$$\n\n \nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{2}{9}.$ Since $p\\le 1,$ the series is divergent.\n\n- For the third series, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{{n}^{3}}}{2{n}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{n}^{3/2}}{{n}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{1/2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Since $p\\le 1,$ the series diverges by the $p$-series test.\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260881 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "76b0ad1e-827e-5795-8431-3276c8eed881" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Values in the Range of the Inverse Sine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3fed7de-1ccc-5e0c-879f-0b3f930e969d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272684} {:answer/id #uuid "ea549571-c1a0-5b0f-9b35-3a62d2bb5829" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272685} {:answer/id #uuid "0671145f-cf46-5f69-8bb9-48b80ff213a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272686} {:answer/id #uuid "cadfc159-190c-552a-85f6-3bf49a01aaf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272687} {:answer/id #uuid "53661af4-a441-5811-96bb-a2052f5f06a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272688}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea549571-c1a0-5b0f-9b35-3a62d2bb5829" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272685} :answer-field/id #uuid "290290cc-275f-50b5-84f2-a87aaab97b22" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274820}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9ba20a3d-72d2-5250-a2dc-c7c364bd7072" :question/math-academy-id "q-65808" :question/problem "Which of the following values is **not** in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/df22eea4331c47da3047381ca672fd80ebf75118054316fe88c0e2d96dae0d34.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The range of the function is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\nTherefore, the value $y=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ is not in the range of the function."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261034 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0adffc7-ef35-5f7a-a790-06addb354c2e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Sum to Infinity of a Geometric Series Given Two of Its Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30da26c9-6495-5c56-b124-e93ef6530a09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272888} {:answer/id #uuid "73483fce-ddf0-5318-8eb9-1f47d2fb3485" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272889} {:answer/id #uuid "cea2049e-3257-5a8a-893d-d49da8f8abe3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272890} {:answer/id #uuid "5094b955-0a0f-5adc-8055-4f8f0f3b1293" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272891} {:answer/id #uuid "78458659-e323-5485-b0bf-ae3e715d3f3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "125" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272892}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78458659-e323-5485-b0bf-ae3e715d3f3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "125" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272892} :answer-field/id #uuid "48aabe8a-9226-56a4-80bb-90d1c91684e6" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274861}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cd3f6efc-8ad1-5ea4-8f11-4ecf2a1e15c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-67275" :question/problem "Consider the positive infinite geometric sequence that has a first term equal to $100$ and a third term equal to $4.$ What is the sum to infinity of the terms of this sequence?" :question/worked-solution "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nFrom the question statement we have ${a}_{1}=100$ and ${a}_{3}=4.$ We can find the common ratio using the following formula:\n\n$$\n{r}^{n-m}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in the known information into the above gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{3-1} & =\\frac{4}{100} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{25} \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{25}} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we have taken the positive square root since we're told that the sequence is positive.\n\nSo, using the formula, we find that the sum to infinity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\infty } & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{100}{(1-\\frac{1}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{100}{(\\frac{4}{5})} \\\\  & =100\\cdot \\frac{5}{4} \\\\  & =125.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261112 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f1fce29-a071-5a86-8a5e-0e7093558d0f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Logarithmic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75311fce-4feb-567e-b92c-49d719962b96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261114} {:answer/id #uuid "c402868e-47ed-50be-b9a7-189ca376d7a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261115} {:answer/id #uuid "77a8eadb-a898-5c1c-ab62-f63855a24e95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261116} {:answer/id #uuid "40e36838-8b21-54b1-8762-5ad5ccff12d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261117} {:answer/id #uuid "d660b1d0-8f74-5e2a-95a1-a3d4bfbb381a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261118} {:answer/id #uuid "722f8723-5569-5bf5-9c17-5140e64ebe19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261119} {:answer/id #uuid "f7bd8e97-4a84-5829-9d91-27806062c05c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261120} {:answer/id #uuid "5162764c-a93f-5b9a-bd1e-2e9c26b3a693" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261121}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d660b1d0-8f74-5e2a-95a1-a3d4bfbb381a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261118} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b376239-9277-5a5a-b27c-bad904bb69b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261113}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "22db072b-47e0-5531-be05-96bd3329aa8c" :question/math-academy-id "q-69791" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y={log}_{5}x?$\n\nI. - The domain is $x>1$\nII. - It has a maximum at $x=1$\nIII. - It passes through the point $(25,2)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y={log}_{5}x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/fd8c4765fa1b07d58a4d0b2e90a07383f5475883a2ada747ea6ae32e7a73808b.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. From the graph, we see that the domain is $x>0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function is strictly increasing on its entire domain. Therefore, there are no maximum values.\n\n- Statement III is true. Substituting $x=25$ into the equation of the curve gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={log}_{5}(25) \\\\  & ={log}_{5}({5}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement III is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261155 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2c88884a-128a-5d46-bfbe-3773139ee11c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Years to Yield a Particular Amount"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41a4bcd9-77e6-5ad9-970e-bc713f05c5d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "94" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273068} {:answer/id #uuid "d2346355-fb9a-534e-83ca-3eaa3620fd4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "92" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273069} {:answer/id #uuid "f9117ef9-e606-544c-9ace-50ae87d75dad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "91" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273070} {:answer/id #uuid "2a071a7d-5437-541c-9aca-9dd9bb90f6b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "93" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273071} {:answer/id #uuid "86efcd3e-152b-5610-afe5-c2f68aa443e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273072}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86efcd3e-152b-5610-afe5-c2f68aa443e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273072} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7cbfb4c-e76e-5764-8ba9-0fdcb08b5b2f" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274897}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cb756b6f-c9b7-51dc-82e2-024fa8b2534a" :question/math-academy-id "q-70210" :question/problem "If you deposit $$2,000$ into an account with a $20%$ annual interest rate, compounded semi-annually, how many years will it take until there is at least $$4,000$ in the account?" :question/worked-solution "The compound interest equation states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & ={P}_{0}{(1+\\frac{r}{n})}^{nt}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe annual interest is compounded semi-annually, so $n=2.$ We also have an initial amount of ${P}_{0}=2,000$ and annual rate of $r=0.2.$\n\nTo find the number of years it will take until $P\\ge 4,000,$ we substitute these values into the formula and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P={P}_{0}{(1+\\frac{r}{n})}^{nt} & \\ge 4,000 \\\\ 2,000{(1+\\frac{0.2}{2})}^{2t} & \\ge 4,000 \\\\ (1+0.1)^{2t} & \\ge \\frac{4,000}{2,000} \\\\ (1.1)^{2t} & \\ge 2 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}({1.1}^{2t}) & \\ge \\operatorname{log}(2) \\\\ 2t\\operatorname{log}(1.1) & \\ge \\operatorname{log}(2) \\\\ 2t & \\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{log}(2)}{\\operatorname{log}(1.1)} \\\\ 2t & \\ge 7.272… \\\\ t & \\ge \\frac{7.272…}{2}\\approx 3.64\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember payments are compounded semi-annually, so we must choose either $3.5$ or $4$ years.\n\n- After $3.5$ years, there is still less than $$4,000$ in the account, because $3.5<3.64.$\n\n- After $4$ years, there will be more than $$4,000$ in the account, since $4>3.64.$\n\nTherefore, it will take $4$ years until there is at least $$4,000$ in the account."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261216 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e88f43c-18c5-5fdb-b6d1-579370489099" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Values in the Domain of the Inverse Sine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2ea99a3-44d9-5b61-9304-0399d7a7a48c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273143} {:answer/id #uuid "15ee2557-2852-53a8-8c69-721a6b0ab293" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5)/(4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273144} {:answer/id #uuid "c7b509a6-8fd3-53cf-a187-a8a061be0b67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273145} {:answer/id #uuid "51d3ef25-db5b-5c6a-8eeb-8f8dc1709b73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273146} {:answer/id #uuid "cdecb957-df84-5bf1-ae67-31774c76c635" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− (1)/(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273147}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2ea99a3-44d9-5b61-9304-0399d7a7a48c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273143} :answer-field/id #uuid "af182ff6-6b4f-5c6e-8b04-1d12975634ea" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274912}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f22b418c-8007-5edd-a5a3-d34d2762b1b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-70620" :question/problem "Which of the following values is **not** in the domain of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/df22eea4331c47da3047381ca672fd80ebf75118054316fe88c0e2d96dae0d34.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The domain of the function is $x\\in [-1,1].$\n\nTherefore, the value $x=\\sqrt{5}$ is not in the domain of the function."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261219 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "67692569-e3a4-51e8-99ac-79d8b3081363" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Points that Lie on the Curve of the Inverse Sine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7326080d-cb11-5bc9-9142-38cca1c83229" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261221} {:answer/id #uuid "23e40e4a-95c6-5c09-b7ed-eb9094ecabc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261222} {:answer/id #uuid "0399991f-5d77-51c3-b1ad-67ea15f6c800" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261223} {:answer/id #uuid "293ad995-8936-53c9-93c7-52ce3ebdf8de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261224} {:answer/id #uuid "08f3c9ef-dfbf-59a9-9a96-c2df57f540a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261225} {:answer/id #uuid "fe618c63-f465-591f-8993-cbc2ddea9f2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261226} {:answer/id #uuid "9622d96d-3ab7-5124-85d2-2533e66ade0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261227} {:answer/id #uuid "92f4f470-f57e-5b0f-b9c3-58b694ebfcb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261228}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92f4f470-f57e-5b0f-b9c3-58b694ebfcb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261228} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee2c0400-eed5-5e93-a306-0c2d0a11b751" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261220}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0a18db1d-9ef4-5e36-9b38-b2a2e715e9c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-70711" :question/problem "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x?$\n\nI. - $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$\nII. - $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$\nIII. - $(\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{\\pi }{6})$" :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}x,$ meaning $a=\\operatorname{sin}b.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x,$ which is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First, we consider the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{1}{2})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}x.$ $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$  does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x$ is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{1}{4})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}x.$ $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{\\pi }{6})$  does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261258 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a71301e9-bc7f-53b9-900e-c3bd37f96b03" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating the Product of a Constant and the Natural Logarithm Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12492260-1d7b-5d4a-920d-d03f74efb3a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3)/(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273163} {:answer/id #uuid "5ee68cda-95ff-5ae5-bd7a-104500693dc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2)/(3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273164} {:answer/id #uuid "edc86000-dd5e-57bc-aae8-22653766d4c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273165} {:answer/id #uuid "1d1fa138-f78f-58ee-ae95-988eff5bc155" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3)/(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273166} {:answer/id #uuid "23364a76-bab6-5c47-8cd0-5c7465bd7251" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273167}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "edc86000-dd5e-57bc-aae8-22653766d4c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273165} :answer-field/id #uuid "efd0927f-17bc-579f-80e4-1c6e6664591d" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274916}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ddbc5046-3110-56db-8516-2c66301e420e" :question/math-academy-id "q-70881" :question/problem "Given that $y=2\\operatorname{ln}x$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The derivative of the natural logarithm is the reciprocal function:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}x)=\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nWe apply our formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\operatorname{ln}x) \\\\  & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}x) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261298 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b194bf2f-feaa-5656-a3ee-0ff466a39e77" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Product Containing a Trigonometric Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c0c2ded-7a42-5245-a213-dca796119f78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x\\sec x\\tan x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273193} {:answer/id #uuid "50de2de2-93bf-5042-bfb0-141b8719d957" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273194} {:answer/id #uuid "66d06710-259c-5f69-9d0b-cbed5858c7e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(2\\tan x + x\\sec^{2} x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273195} {:answer/id #uuid "11af0f2c-99c4-5f2f-93da-85192de21048" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sec x(2 + x^{2}\\tan x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273196} {:answer/id #uuid "2b90ca82-9b81-5d05-b24e-2832850dc8ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\tan x(2x + \\sec x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273197}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50de2de2-93bf-5042-bfb0-141b8719d957" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273194} :answer-field/id #uuid "3aee7b73-82b9-5fb0-aad3-c0e8fdfd456d" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274922}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "568c0119-02eb-5eba-9b08-2993ed865711" :question/math-academy-id "q-71004" :question/problem "Differentiate ${e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)={e}^{x}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{sin}x.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}x)^{'} & ={u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x) \\\\  & =({e}^{x})^{'}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}x+{e}^{x}\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}x)^{'} \\\\  & =({e}^{x})(\\operatorname{sin}x)+({e}^{x})(\\operatorname{cos}x) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}x\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261380 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e04791d-e117-5a87-8491-1b2b5797f13a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Initial Deposit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f1a78b0-1fc8-5383-978e-646417bb4c2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(5946)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273312} {:answer/id #uuid "f3ef7bda-1a8c-5dfe-acc2-0513a5e082f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(5946)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273313} {:answer/id #uuid "4f9f8172-e569-5f7f-a87f-15edbd4dbb55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5946" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273314} {:answer/id #uuid "9b7d7dfc-6376-5ae7-abf9-64bb1fe07d53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(5946)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273315} {:answer/id #uuid "b37e0a72-340e-5d0e-9fd9-61215a8bb437" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(5946)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273316}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f9f8172-e569-5f7f-a87f-15edbd4dbb55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5946" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273314} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f1b8e27-4233-59e4-b23a-6a4c01b0e8cc" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274946}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5c0c9df4-4d82-539b-8226-ce3e2837f6e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-72243" :question/problem "How much money would you need to deposit today at a $10%$ annual interest rate, compounded yearly, to have $$40,000$ in the account after $20$ years? Round your answer to the nearest dollar." :question/worked-solution "The compound interest equation states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & ={P}_{0}{(1+\\frac{r}{n})}^{nt}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe annual interest is compounded once per year, so we have $n=1.$ We also have an annual rate of $r=0.1,$ and a final amount of $P=40,000$ after $t=20$ years. To find the initial deposit, ${P}_{0},$ we substitute these values into the formula and solve for ${P}_{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & ={P}_{0}{(1+\\frac{r}{n})}^{nt} \\\\ 40,000 & ={P}_{0}{(1+\\frac{0.1}{1})}^{1(20)} \\\\ 40,000 & ={P}_{0}(1.1)^{20} \\\\ 40,000 & ={P}_{0}(6.727…) \\\\ {P}_{0} & =\\frac{40,000}{6.727…} \\\\ {P}_{0} & \\approx 5,946\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, you would need to deposit $$5,946.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261786 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ead28b84-1f92-510d-87b3-07eb9a394622" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a p-Series with Integer Exponent Converges"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "272b5379-c765-5807-9d72-291fa23d8916" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273755} {:answer/id #uuid "677d02a6-dcba-5369-91e3-88be44980fa4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273756} {:answer/id #uuid "556a5c7e-d467-5712-ba47-ce94694ee9ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273757} {:answer/id #uuid "a11a484c-6f0f-5674-a43d-a6e87c00da7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273758} {:answer/id #uuid "39d36126-da4d-5999-9b66-f31ec0224424" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273759}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a11a484c-6f0f-5674-a43d-a6e87c00da7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273758} :answer-field/id #uuid "54719b85-f3e4-5209-abe1-e21628ff7c1c" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275035}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cf874b53-ef0a-5fdf-9ef3-0a8f809bc7de" :question/math-academy-id "q-81428" :question/problem "Use the $p$-test to determine whether the series below converges or diverges.\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{n}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The series can be written as\n\n$$\n8{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n}.\n$$\n\nThis is a $p$-series with $p=1$ (the harmonic series). Since $p\\le 1,$ the series is divergent.\n\nNote that the factor of $8$ multiplying the series has no effect on the convergence/divergence of the series."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261872 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6051f35-c390-57ba-8448-40f53f11c1c1" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Which Series Converge From a List"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c79b06e-20b6-545a-bf3f-4ace475d5b58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261874} {:answer/id #uuid "8fabf757-29a7-5810-8ad6-bd173bea819e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261875} {:answer/id #uuid "4c35feee-56b7-527a-9eee-d30c114b58ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261876} {:answer/id #uuid "b3815365-f016-5d77-9ce7-512dc1e1c99f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261877} {:answer/id #uuid "cd21ecf6-cc48-56ea-bc16-ba8db7b8c72a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261878} {:answer/id #uuid "906d8a92-c585-5a67-8fad-0f5a15fe64dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261879} {:answer/id #uuid "d22f00a5-7b8e-54b3-958e-6974ad2ff725" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261880} {:answer/id #uuid "ceb00b21-f4d3-516a-a25b-c63f9d245980" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261881}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8fabf757-29a7-5810-8ad6-bd173bea819e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261875} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa99ed83-7eac-5c53-a422-b00ad94c2731" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261873}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "59b096bf-4af9-5e57-941d-caa731cefc46" :question/math-academy-id "q-83593" :question/problem "Which of the following series are convergent?\n\nI. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4n}$\nII. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}\\sqrt{n}}$\nIII. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{3{n}^{5}}$" :question/worked-solution "We analyze each series in turn.\n\n- First, we look at ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4n}.$ Rewriting this series gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4n}=\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n},\n$$\n\n \nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=1$ (the harmonic series). Since $p\\le 1,$ the series is divergent.\n\n- For the second series, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}\\sqrt{n}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}\\cdot {n}^{1/2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{5/2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand this is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{5}{2}.$ Since $p>1,$ the series converges by the $p$-series test.\n\n- For the third series, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{3{n}^{5}} & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{5}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand this is a $p$-series with $p=5.$ Since $p>1,$ the series converges by the $p$-series test.\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261977 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f975e5c-637e-57f6-a193-68254799e79b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Sum of Convergent Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cafa79d-c509-50aa-88fa-0cd0899a90a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273948} {:answer/id #uuid "848d3b02-7c0b-5c65-9269-c159d5b384c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273949} {:answer/id #uuid "547d3ff3-893c-554b-b52a-d42fe9cb9ad9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273950} {:answer/id #uuid "c5302293-2286-5d63-a225-77a1c561f932" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273951} {:answer/id #uuid "1b60ee30-3bc8-5642-932a-443034d46c2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273952}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b60ee30-3bc8-5642-932a-443034d46c2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273952} :answer-field/id #uuid "8577df57-939b-56d8-a9c2-c9bf55d66724" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275074}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "770c1ff4-046b-5d4d-9ce0-c706d161310b" :question/math-academy-id "q-9293" :question/problem "If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}=0.5,\\,$ ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}=9$ and ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{c}_{n}=-2,$ then calculate ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3{a}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }2{b}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8{c}_{n}.$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the constant factors outside of the summation signs gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3{a}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }2{b}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8{c}_{n} & =3{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}+2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}+8{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{c}_{n} \\\\  & =3(0.5)+2(9)+8(-2) \\\\  & =1.5+18-16 \\\\  & =3.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262001 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "855a9c5c-9d01-584b-9b24-99834365b732" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a p-Series With Fractional Exponent Converges"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e60278a6-e78d-5714-82c9-42bc990c5a7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273988} {:answer/id #uuid "f9aaf13f-be85-5af3-9ced-38667858b3cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273989} {:answer/id #uuid "82552eaa-ae5c-5356-97dd-116ac750da54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273990} {:answer/id #uuid "05de0f5b-5de0-56e8-b0c5-72444449153e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273991} {:answer/id #uuid "e64cede5-ca09-5ec9-b9d7-3ce3e188e8ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273992}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82552eaa-ae5c-5356-97dd-116ac750da54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273990} :answer-field/id #uuid "65c44e5b-cbcd-5c4d-b787-1496cc2a11b3" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275082}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e09514d4-1e05-5fb3-ba5a-f0e56cf060c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-9332" :question/problem "Determine whether the series\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt[8]{{n}^{7}}}$\nconverges or diverges." :question/worked-solution "The series can be written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{7/8}},\n$$\n\nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{7}{8}.$ Since $p\\le 1$, the series is divergent. \n\nNote that the factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ multiplying the series has no effect on the convergence/divergence of the series."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262004 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "855a9c5c-9d01-584b-9b24-99834365b732" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a p-Series With Fractional Exponent Converges"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e9ddef1-1b24-5317-8aee-d24d1b0f8d6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273993} {:answer/id #uuid "3eb38993-642b-5a53-ad61-678fd5b51a14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273994} {:answer/id #uuid "0a887442-8084-5775-9b57-6f79f5a82a90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273995} {:answer/id #uuid "7f8b15e7-e880-5218-b4af-a21188e2d29e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273996} {:answer/id #uuid "be1db7fb-699b-5022-a0f7-1640ff78037e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273997}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f8b15e7-e880-5218-b4af-a21188e2d29e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273996} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f1531f2-efc2-5b1a-9b92-4e2b482b41f2" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275083}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "47306eda-69a1-5c4c-86bf-6d6e220f28a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-9334" :question/problem "Determine whether the series\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{{n}^{3}}}$\nconverges or diverges." :question/worked-solution "The series can be written as\n\n$$\n3{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{3/2}},\n$$\n\nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{3}{2}.$ Since $p>1$, the series is convergent. \n\nNote that the factor of $3$ multiplying the series has no effect on the convergence/divergence of the series."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262043 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fa0c4b2-8dc6-5823-a9bb-b933a9f9ee7f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether an Ordinary Differential Equation is Autonomous or Nonautonomous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af8323ab-ddb1-5f48-a9f7-f5692ae339da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262045} {:answer/id #uuid "f8784524-b57e-5054-9fa6-028c173f4c6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262046} {:answer/id #uuid "e07ee293-119a-578f-9cb9-bdb3e5415610" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262047} {:answer/id #uuid "d24cd77e-16ed-5f5a-9c5e-0234683e143e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262048} {:answer/id #uuid "c2d72649-3668-5f78-ada6-9565b30f7fe3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262049} {:answer/id #uuid "dd234ff3-f83e-5cf9-87fe-e14c16f76f18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262050} {:answer/id #uuid "a2cfd61a-0a0f-5f14-900b-5550cae3912a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262051} {:answer/id #uuid "06e6c92a-97d1-564d-863d-bda507afccfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262052}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06e6c92a-97d1-564d-863d-bda507afccfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262052} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f7fab41-ea7c-53fd-85ac-6541129d2e5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262044}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6d484918-7854-547b-b753-257beeb0c7d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-96702" :question/problem "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nI. - $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-x={e}^{y}$\nII. - $y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2=\\sqrt{y}$\nIII. - $4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{x}{y}=5y$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{'}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-x & ={e}^{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{y}+x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)={e}^{y}+x.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2 & =\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{y}+2}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\frac{\\sqrt{y}+2}{2}.$\n\n- Equation III is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{x}{y} & =5y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{4}(5y+\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{4}(5y+\\frac{x}{y}).$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262073 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fa0c4b2-8dc6-5823-a9bb-b933a9f9ee7f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether an Ordinary Differential Equation is Autonomous or Nonautonomous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60ed9cbb-4ba4-51aa-a0f8-7225e1688c36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262075} {:answer/id #uuid "31036b5b-7449-5599-8b7f-8b39b778f239" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262076} {:answer/id #uuid "be9955de-2d2b-52bd-ad19-a9c11284301d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262077} {:answer/id #uuid "c053f2d6-9364-55b3-954f-91552728a08d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262078} {:answer/id #uuid "8163967d-e6d8-56ce-9dca-756d234c3020" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262079} {:answer/id #uuid "11bda8cf-ada4-544f-8e86-30b984916eaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262080} {:answer/id #uuid "f1a652c4-ff96-5e6d-b026-86d6eed744a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262081} {:answer/id #uuid "9e078445-c2fc-5a31-88ab-c2be941bd0f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262082}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8163967d-e6d8-56ce-9dca-756d234c3020" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262079} :answer-field/id #uuid "831ff5c2-b840-5fe4-b6ea-ec995d5e6729" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262074}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a6f5f12c-8742-57e1-8431-b7e5f7c8b33f" :question/math-academy-id "q-96712" :question/problem "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nI. - ${y}^{'}=1-\\sqrt{y}$\nII. - ${y}^{'}-\\operatorname{tan}y={e}^{2}$\nIII. - $x{y}^{'}-\\frac{1}{y}=4$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{'}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. It is of the form ${y}^{'}=f(y),$ where $f(y)=1-\\sqrt{y}.$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{'}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'}-\\operatorname{tan}y & ={e}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{'} & =\\operatorname{tan}y+{e}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{'} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\operatorname{tan}y+{e}^{2}.$\n\n- Equation III is *not* an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{'}-\\frac{1}{y} & =4 \\\\ {y}^{'} & =\\frac{4y+1}{xy} \\\\ {y}^{'} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\frac{4y+1}{xy}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{'}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262093 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "13e08fd0-4f41-56eb-8650-b72aa739b7a1" :knowledge-point/title "Initial Value Problems and Initial Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "491c18dc-e9d5-5a2b-88f5-a3bc86a065ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262095} {:answer/id #uuid "6cee0851-d168-5175-af4b-4e9ec6593fd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262096} {:answer/id #uuid "e20c0aff-74cc-552d-96eb-eef3a81e96ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262097} {:answer/id #uuid "f0377b83-e245-52ea-80a0-298ddfe36899" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262098} {:answer/id #uuid "c6bcccdb-85b9-52d9-b7ca-5d988b9f7fb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262099} {:answer/id #uuid "070e48b3-a56e-5c40-9343-941d4858140e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262100} {:answer/id #uuid "40d7f3bf-ff28-581c-96a7-f1b98edbccd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262101} {:answer/id #uuid "f1223c20-84d2-5fb5-bf8a-545a08f3d710" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262102}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "070e48b3-a56e-5c40-9343-941d4858140e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262100} :answer-field/id #uuid "36b80bee-db64-535d-aace-058b279199ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262094}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f324e71f-0765-5434-b640-0e2263e787be" :question/math-academy-id "q-96992" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\nI. - $3{x}^{2}{y}^{'}={y}^{2},\\,y(0)=1$\nII. - $\\frac{{y}^{'}}{2}-{x}^{2}{y}^{2}=y,\\,y(1)=2$\nIII. - ${y}^{'}-x\\sqrt{y}={e}^{x}$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{'}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $3{x}^{2}{y}^{'}={y}^{2}$ together with the initial condition $y(0)=1.$\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $\\frac{{y}^{'}}{2}-{x}^{2}{y}^{2}=y$ together with the initial condition $y(1)=2.$\n\n- System III  is not a first-order initial value problem. We have a first-order ordinary differential equation, but there is no initial condition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262130 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c547746-49c8-5a4a-85fe-c6b75493a354" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Quotient Containing Other Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37e7421e-e65c-5b2c-b3a6-14a17c8a475a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(-\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274043} {:answer/id #uuid "3bebd927-dd62-52a9-b22e-be9cf8df02c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274044} {:answer/id #uuid "cc6a369a-2941-556f-ae3d-8060dac4d25d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274045} {:answer/id #uuid "0841a336-134f-56ea-8ce0-0bdecd2f16bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(-\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274046} {:answer/id #uuid "cbe67c23-2872-5fb7-95dd-9a325b50a7da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(-\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274047}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3bebd927-dd62-52a9-b22e-be9cf8df02c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274044} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e3ae093-2eb9-5104-9a9f-42cd7fdd0cc0" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275093}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c1aeb34a-99a8-5372-b85e-e163f31127c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-9908" :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x}{1+\\operatorname{sin}x},$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, the expression for $y$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)=\\operatorname{cos}x$ and $v(x)=1+\\operatorname{sin}x.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}x)^{'}\\cdot (1+\\operatorname{sin}x)-\\operatorname{cos}x\\cdot (1+\\operatorname{sin}x)^{'}}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\operatorname{sin}x)(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)-(\\operatorname{cos}x)\\operatorname{cos}x}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\operatorname{sin}x-{sin}^{2}x-{cos}^{2}x)}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\operatorname{sin}x-({sin}^{2}x+{cos}^{2}x))}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Since ${sin}^{2}x+{cos}^{2}x=1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{(-\\operatorname{sin}x-1)}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{1+\\operatorname{sin}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-\\frac{1}{1+\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{1+1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $-\\frac{1}{2}.$"}]}
